  "content": {
    "title": "Six Flags America That Shut Down After 50 Years Will Find a New Purpose Thanks to Kevin Durant",
    "text": "NEED TO KNOWKevin Durant's 35V and Atlanta-based TPA Group purchased the 515-acre site of the former Six Flags America park in Prince George's County, Md.The park, which closed in November 2022, operated for over 50 years and employed about 70 full-time workers.County officials envision a mixed-use entertainment site with upscale dining and sports-related activities to boost year-round revenue.The former site of Six Flags America may be about to get a new life, thanks in part to NBA star Kevin Durant.On Wednesday, April 8, Maryland's Prince George's County Executive Aisha Braveboy announced a new ownership team for the former Six Flags property, located about 20 miles from Washington, D.C. The 515-acre site was sold to Durant's 35V and the Atlanta-based TPA Group.“This is a new chapter for Prince George's County and one that will be guided by a son of Prince George's County, NBA legend Kevin Durant,” Braveboy said during Wednesday's press conference. “We are excited about being able to work collaboratively with Wanda Durant (Kevin's mother) and the team to really have an inclusive vision for the Six Flags site.”Kevin Durant dribbles the ball during a match against the Minnesota Timberwolves on March 25 in Minneapolis.Credit: David Berding/GettyAccording to local outlet WJLA, the 37-year-old Houston Rockets forward is a native of the D.C. area. His family's companies, including the Durant Family Foundation, are known to have hosted a number of charity events for youth in the DMV.The news comes five months after Six Flags, North America's largest regional amusement park operator, closed the property permanently last fall. After more than 50 years of operation, Six Flags America and the Hurricane Harbor water park in Bowie, Md., shut their gates on Sunday, Nov. 2.The park first opened in 1974 as a drive-thru safari known as The Wildlife Preserve. Eventually it was converted into a Six Flags property in 1999.Signage at Six Flags America in May 2025Credit: Robb Hill for The Washington Post via GettyIn a Six Flags press release announcing the closure, the company noted it employed about 70 full-time associates. At the time, Six Flags said severance and other benefits would be provided to eligible employees.The company attributed the closure to Six Flags' ongoing efforts to restructure and address ongoing debt. \"After reviewing a number of options, we believe that marketing the property for redevelopment will generate the highest value and return on investment,” former company president and CEO Richard A. Zimmerman said in a May 1 press release.Six Flags has since announced it's selling seven other underperforming theme parks.On April 6, the company shared it had successfully closed on the sale of six U.S. parks, transferring ownership to Kansas City-based real estate investment trust EPR Properties. The new owners plan to partner with Enchanted Parks to operate the properties, while maintaining Six Flags branding, through the end of the year.Never miss a story — sign up for PEOPLE's free daily newsletter to stay up-to-date on the best of what PEOPLE has to offer​​, from celebrity news to compelling human interest stories.While exact plans for the Maryland site were not announced, county officials hope a new development would operate year-round instead of seasonally to bring in more tax dollars. Braveboy hinted she's looking for a mixed-use entertainment site with upscale dining and possibly sports-related activities.“What's most important is that the community voice will play a central role as we move forward,” Braveboy said. “In collaboration with the new ownership team, I will co-host a series of engagement events to ensure residents have the opportunity to share their expectations, inform the planning process and help shape the future of this important site.”She added: “Our residents deserve to be a part of what comes next.”Read the original article on People",
